one of the most important pieces to the  persona series and a major reason why we  remember each game so fondly is the  soundtrack each main line persona game  and spin-off has their own memorable  songs that encapsulate their defining  moments and with persona 3 dancing in  moonlight and persona 5 dancing in  starlight those evocative soundtracks  are brought to the forefront for fun  exhilarating rhythm games with their own  charming personalities taking center  stage  [Music]  both use the rythm gameplay system first  established in persona 4 dancing all  night six button inputs bordered the  screen and notes flow from the center  out to the corresponding input double  notes holds and DJ scratches with the  flick of an analog stick keep you on  your toes which makes things  delightfully hectic on the highest  difficulties  it's an incredibly fun rhythm system  that's supported by note patterns that  flow seamlessly with the beautiful track  lists there's an undeniable satisfaction  to nailing perfect combos as the claps  tambourines and scratches sync with the  beat of the song it's not far off to say  that you feel the rhythm but note  patterns will come naturally as you grow  familiar with each track all the while  the casts of each game dance with  impressive fluidity especially with how  partner and group dances are  choreographed with a natural  imperfection  everyone's expressive and going all-out  whether it's the phantom thieves dancing  deep into a hostile palace or seas  getting down atop Tartarus during the  dark hour  [Music]  now I'll be the first to admit that  there's a thematic dissonance between  dancing and flashy outfits and persona  three's darker tone which might be  off-putting for die-hard fans of that  game still the fact that we see these  old locations recreated with modern  visuals is a trip you were taught I  station polonium all the get coke on  high entrance and rooftop Tartarus it's  all here and it's an absolute delight to  be with the Seas crew again reincarnated  and 3d models in the same vein as  persona 5 art style  it's a reimagining of yukari's joyful  demeanor Mitsuru Stern attitude and June  pays goofiness I do wish we had Shinji  from the start but he's available as DLC  and sadly car Omar was missing but it's  an overwhelming feeling like seeing old  friends in a new light most of all  dancing in moonlight stands out with a  tracklist that spans the course of  persona threes history which includes  songs from persona 3 fests persona queue  and persona 3 portable it gives a  musical variety that's attached to so  many great memories but that's also  exciting to play the remixes and  remasters evoke not just a sense of  nostalgia but have a newfound quality  that breathes new life into persona 3  particularly songs like heartful cry  when the moon reaches for the stars burn  my dread memories of you the list goes  on  dancing in moonlight carries this  powerful legacy  [Music]  when it comes to persona 5 dancing at  starlight the swagger and style of the  phantom thieves bursts at the scenes its  scene in a wink and a nod as they move  in a way that fits their personalities  they'll be getting down in familiar  locations like mementos Shibuya crossing  and shootin academy in the theme song  groovy that's so beautifully drawn and  animated you can't help but feel that  they had the moves in them a number of  great songs like rivers in the desert  blooming villain and life goes on are  featured here and of course the best  Palestine price has Makoto throwing it  down in front of the bank in the sky  [Music]  the wonderful fusion of jazz metal and  downtempo make for a great playlist that  feels like a trip through the struggles  and triumphs of persona 5 all over again  there are a few decent remixes though  they don't shine as brightly as they do  in persona 3 there are also a few  shortcomings and dancing in starlight  when it comes to presentation life will  change an empowering song with  infectious confidence is paired with a  fairly cheesy music video more  importantly it's unfortunate that the  female cast gets over sexualized in the  last surprise music video which is some  sort of bizarre burlesque show that's  out of touch out of place and wholly  unnecessary  [Music]  unlike persona 4 dancing all night there  is no traditional story mode in either  game outside of dancing both games  features social events where  conversations with your squad take place  the premise is that they're stuck in a  dream state where your velvet room  representative asks you to dance it's  silly but it's enough to provide the  context necessary for the social events  a series of neat scenes where characters  simply banter there isn't much in the  way of new character development aside  from their newfound determination to  dance social events play off of what you  already know about the cast's akiko is  still obsessed with getting strong and I  guess is still working her way around  typical human mannerisms on striving to  be the next top model and use Ches  enraptured by his artistic side  regardless it's nice to chill with them  again and watch them go back and forth  with each other especially since the  original voice cast returned for both  games social events also provide  incentives for playing the game in  different ways each character has  specific conditions for unlocking their  social events like passing songs using  certain modifiers or writing several  outfits or accessories during your time  playing it's well worth it especially  for the room visits even if it's just a  dorm room or the attic of LeBlanc coffee  shop visiting these places in  first-person breathes new life into  worlds you thought you knew each game  has 23 tracks and eight short social  events for every crew member but it's so  easy to get lost in these songs both old  and new memories of the original RPGs  and all the games in between come  flooding back  they're also annoyed  their own right like the game itself the  opening theme our moment is a  celebration of persona 3 and the journey  with C's despite its dark undertones the  unapologetic confidence and style of  persona 5 s phantom thieves comes  through vividly and their own theme song  that's inspiring all over again  these unique soundtracks take center  stage and showcase the incredible work  of series composer Shoji maguro and  company the rhythm gameplay system and  the songs are perfect matches  but there's something particularly  special about persona 3 dancing and  moonlight it's in the suite remixes and  remasters of familiar songs recreations  of places we've been and the  re-imagination of characters we've long  known I'm also not going to overlook a  chance to revisit the Phantom thieves in  persona 5 dancing at starlight you may  find the overall premise a little  strange and you won't find character  development beyond what's already been  established in the past but if you let  loose just as the cast have done for  these games you'll find brilliant fun  rhythm games and some of the best songs  ever  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  you
 

